action-units-bea88783.interpretation:
The dimensional analysis of Action 
[S]
 reveals its structure as 
 M L^2 T^{-1}
. This means Action has units of mass times length squared divided by time. This dimensional form can be recognized as the product of energy (which has units of 
 M L^2 T^{-2}
) and time (
 T
), or equivalently, as the product of momentum (
 M L T^{-1}
) and length (
 L
). In classical mechanics, the action is defined as the integral of the Lagrangian (
 L = T - V
, where 
 T
 is kinetic energy and 
 V
 is potential energy) with respect to time: 
 S = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} L dt = \int_{t_1}^{t_2} (T - V) dt
. The principle of least action states that the actual path taken by a system between two points in configuration space is the one for which the action is stationary (usually a minimum).
